<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>For each pair of events, decide whether they are mutually exclusive, independent, or neither.</p><p>(a) A die is rolled once. Event A: it shows 2. Event B: it shows 5.</p><div>A and B are [ mutually exclusive | independent | neither ]  </div><p>(b) A coin is tossed and a die is rolled. Event A: the coin shows heads. Event B: the die shows 6.</p><div>A and B are [ mutually exclusive | independent | neither ]  </div><p>(c) A die is rolled once. Event A: the score is even. Event B: the score is less than 4.</p><div>A and B are [ mutually exclusive | independent | neither ]  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Ask two questions. Can both happen at the same time? If not, the events are mutually exclusive. Does one happening change the probability of the other? If not, they are independent. Mutually exclusive events that can each happen are never independent: if one happens, the other cannot.</p><p>(a) Mutually exclusive. If the die shows 2, it cannot show 5, so knowing one changes the probability of the other to 0. They are mutually exclusive, and so not independent. Can the die show 2 and 5 at once? No, so the events are mutually exclusive.</p><p>(b) Independent. The coin and the die do not affect each other: the die shows 6 with probability one in six whatever the coin shows. They are independent. Both can happen together, for example heads and a 6, so they are not mutually exclusive.</p><p>(c) Neither. They are linked: the probability of an even score is one half, but of the scores less than 4 (1, 2 and 3) only one in three is even. So they are not independent. Both can happen together: a score of 2 is even and less than 4.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p>mutually exclusive</p></answer>
    <answer><p>independent</p></answer>
    <answer><p>neither</p></answer>
</stack-static>